Name: move file to trash

Type: command

Syntax: move file <pathname> to trash

Summary:
Moves a <file> to the system Trash or Recycle Bin.

Introduced: 1.0.0

OS: mac, windows, linux

Platforms: desktop

Security: disk

Example:
move file "/Users/me/Desktop/old-notes.txt" to trash

Example:
move file (tFilePath) to trash
if the result is not empty then
   answer "Could not move file to trash: " & the result
end if

Parameters:
pathname:
Specifies the name and location of the file. If you specify a name but
not a location, the engine looks for the file in the defaultFolder.

The result:
If the file does not exist or cannot be moved to the trash for any
reason, the result is set to "can't delete that file".

Description:
Use the <move file to trash> <command> to remove a file in a way that
allows the user to recover it later. Unlike <delete file>, this command
does not permanently remove the file — it moves it to the platform's
Trash or Recycle Bin, from which the user can restore or permanently
delete it at their discretion.

On macOS, the file is moved using the system's standard trash mechanism,
which correctly handles files on external volumes.

On Windows, the file is sent to the Recycle Bin using the shell's file
operation API.

On Linux, the command first attempts to use `gio trash` if available.
If `gio` is not found, it falls back to the XDG Trash specification,
moving the file to `~/.local/share/Trash/files/` and writing the
corresponding `.trashinfo` metadata file.

References: move folder to trash (command), delete file (command),
delete folder (command), file (keyword), command (glossary)

Tags: file system
